course: dictionary
lesson: 13341
title: Getting used to things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To habituate something means to make yourself, or someone else, used to it by meeting it again and again. «After a week on the new street, Maya had habituated to the traffic.» The clues are "after a week" and "used to", so habituate is about becoming familiar with something over time.
explain: Habituate is a formal word, so you will meet it in books, reports and science lessons. When you see it, ask: what happened many times? In the sentence above, the traffic kept happening, and Maya stopped noticing it.
explain: In science, habituation is a simple kind of learning. A bird may fly off when a scarecrow first appears, but after many days it stops reacting, because nothing bad happens. The bird has become habituated to the scarecrow.
fact: Habituate means to make someone or something used to something by doing or meeting it again and again.
fact: «Habituated» is the past form, as in «The birds habituated to the scarecrow.»
fact: «Habituates» is used with he, she or it, and «habituating» is the -ing form.
fact: Scientists call it habituation when an animal stops responding to something harmless that keeps happening.
fact: Wild animals such as bears and foxes can become habituated to people, and then they may become dangerous, so never feed them.
exercise: «After a week by the busy road, Sam had habituated to the traffic noise.» What does habituated mean here? | Ran away from the noise | Heard the noise for the very first time | *Became used to the noise and stopped noticing it | Grew angry at the noise
exercise: Which word means to make someone used to something by doing or meeting it again and again? | forget | *habituate | wander | scare
exercise: Why should you never feed a wild fox that has become habituated to people? | Because food always makes foxes sleepy | *Because it may lose its fear of people and become dangerous | Because it will then fly away for ever | Because foxes only eat grass
test: The birds habituated to the scarecrow after many days, because nothing bad ever happened. Why did they stop flying away? | Because they were hungry | Because they were scared of it | *Because it kept happening and never harmed them | Because the scarecrow was asleep
test: Which sentence uses habituate correctly? | Ella habituated quickly and then ran to the shop | The bird habituated its sandwich in the pond | *The new puppy habituated to the sound of the vacuum cleaner after many days | Tom habituated a cake in the oven
exam: Jayden says habituate means to hate something. Is he right? | No, it means to forget something completely | *No, it means to become used to something by meeting it again and again | Yes, it means to run away from something scary | Yes, it means to dislike something very much
